# Rule Authoring

## Rule File Format

Every .mdc rule file must have YAML frontmatter with:
- description: short purpose and when to use the rule.
- globs: file patterns that trigger the rule (optional).
- alwaysApply: true only for short, universal workflow rules.

Example:

```yaml
---
description: Short purpose and when to use the rule.
globs:
  - "**/*api*"
alwaysApply: false
---
```

## Sizing Guidelines

- Keep each rule file under 160 lines.
- Keep AGENTS.md under 60 lines.
- Use alwaysApply: true sparingly — only for the workflow rule.
- Move detailed references to doc files, not rules.

## Rule Hierarchy

1. AI_DIRECTIVES.md overrides all other instruction sources.
2. AGENTS.md overrides .cursor/rules/*.mdc.
3. Scoped rules override inline code comments.
4. If two rules conflict, follow the more restrictive one and record the
   conflict in CURRENT_TASKS.md.

## .rulesrc.yaml Maintenance

- Update selection globs when new file patterns emerge.
- Update enabled_areas when the project scope changes.
- Verify that every load path points to an existing .mdc file.
- Use conditional document loading to reduce context cost.

## Adding New Rules

1. Identify the domain and file patterns.
2. Check whether an existing rule already covers the area.
3. Create the .mdc file with proper frontmatter.
4. Add the selection entry in .rulesrc.yaml.
5. Verify the frontmatter parses as valid YAML.
6. Keep the rule under 160 lines.

## Anti-Patterns

- Do not duplicate content between AGENTS.md and AI_DIRECTIVES.md.
- Do not repeat risk-tier definitions in rule files — reference
  AI_DIRECTIVES.md instead.
- Do not use alwaysApply: true for domain-specific rules.
- Do not exceed 160 lines; split into separate rules if needed.
